{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a6bf1fe5c7706bdf3","tool_use_id":"toolu_01E5qdXDrgD7MQ7cvXgf66Ae","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":72480,"tool_uses":19,"duration_ms":168993},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"c52465a1-7ef1-4ebb-af7d-366cba80faa1","session_id":"8810d8ca-95df-4cbf-a0e9-6349587d15d7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017KiQGWpdPrUPhDsvohttuN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0194y6kaWZ43VH3ivGokanW2","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"schema_semantic_constrained_ie","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T094226","overall_score":"4 - Weak Accept","report":"# Reviewer R1: Methodology and Experimental Rigor\n\n**Paper:** \"How LLMs Violate Ontology Constraints: A Landscape Study and Grammar-Based Mitigation\" (submitted as OntoCoDec)\n\n**Overall Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThe paper studies how LLMs violate ontology type constraints in zero-shot information extraction, proposes a two-layer constrained decoding framework (grammar + semantic), and evaluates on SciERC (7 relations) and DocRED (96 relations) with two models (Qwen3-8B, Llama-3.1-8B). The grammar layer eliminates entity-type and relation-vocabulary violations universally. The semantic layer reduces domain/range (DR) violations by 94% on SciERC (15.4% to 0.93%) and 46% on DocRED (19.0% to 10.19%), at zero F1 cost but with elevated cross-field consistency (CF) violations. A tightness-violation correlation analysis provides a predictive framework for constraint effectiveness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Methodology Assessment\n\n### 2.1 Violation Taxonomy\nThe four-violation taxonomy (ET, RV, DR, CF) is well-defined and maps cleanly to different levels of constraint expressiveness. The decidability taxonomy (purely local / prefix-decidable with state / truly non-local) is a useful conceptual contribution that correctly identifies which constraints can be enforced at what cost. However:\n- The taxonomy omits **cardinality constraints** (e.g., functional relations where only one tail is valid) and **inverse constraints**, which are common in real ontologies. The paper should acknowledge this scope limitation.\n- CF violations are defined as \"same entity assigned different types across relations,\" but the paper does not clarify whether this is measured per-document or globally, and whether entity identity is established by exact string match or fuzzy match.\n\n### 2.2 Grammar Compilation\nThe grammar layer approach is sound and well-motivated. Compiling ontology schemas into CFGs via XGrammar for vocabulary and structural enforcement is a natural extension of existing work. The ET/RV elimination results (0% across datasets and models) validate this approach convincingly. The 14% latency overhead is practical.\n\n### 2.3 Semantic Constraint Layer\nThe semantic layer design is reasonable as a proof-of-concept but has a fundamental architectural limitation that the paper partially acknowledges:\n- **Per-relation independent type enforcement** is the root cause of CF violations. The entity registry tracks entity types as they are generated, but when the semantic layer enforces domain/range constraints for relation R, it may assign an entity type that conflicts with what was assigned for relation R'. This is not merely a \"type system ambiguity\" issue -- it is a design decision with predictable consequences.\n- The **graceful degradation** fallback (when semantic mask intersects grammar mask = empty set) is presented as rare (1/998 DocRED docs), but its frequency is a function of constraint tightness. On ontologies with even tighter constraints, fallback frequency could dominate, effectively nullifying the semantic layer. The paper provides no analysis of this scaling behavior.\n\n### 2.4 Constraint Tightness Metric\nThe tightness metric (Eq. 1) is simple and interpretable: $1 - |E_h^r| \\cdot |E_t^r| / |E|^2$. However, it treats all type pairs as equally likely, ignoring the model's distributional prior over entity types. A \"tightness\" metric weighted by the model's type distribution would better predict violations. The current metric's moderate correlation (rho=0.52) on DocRED likely reflects this limitation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Experimental Rigor\n\n### 3.1 Baselines\n- **Fair:** The unconstrained baseline uses the same pipeline (Phase 2) as constrained experiments, ensuring fair comparison. Grammar-only and semantic-only ablations cleanly isolate each layer's contribution.\n- **Missing:** No comparison to post-hoc filtering baselines. The paper argues post-hoc filtering \"wastes compute\" and \"cannot redirect probability mass,\" but does not measure the actual F1/DR trade-off of post-hoc rejection + regeneration. This would strengthen the argument for decode-time enforcement.\n- **Missing:** No comparison to training-time constraint enforcement (e.g., constrained loss functions, KnowCoder-style approaches). While the paper targets zero-shot settings, demonstrating that OntoCoDec complements or matches fine-tuned approaches would be valuable.\n\n### 3.2 Ablation Study\nThe ablation is well-structured for SciERC (unconstrained / grammar-only / semantic-only / full S1 / full S2). For DocRED, the semantic-only condition is included. The enabling dependency analysis (enabling ratio 3.88x SciERC, 2.52x DocRED) is a good quantitative characterization of the grammar-semantic interaction.\n\nHowever:\n- The S2 (two-pass) strategy is under-analyzed. The paper dismisses it quickly, but S2 was designed to address CF violations. The fact that it fails at this deserves more investigation -- does S2 produce different CF patterns, or simply fewer triples?\n- No ablation on **prompt design** interacting with constrained decoding. The paper shows schema-informed prompting alone reduces DR by 62%, but never tests schema-informed prompting + constrained decoding together. This combination might yield better results than minimal prompt + constrained decoding.\n\n### 3.3 Statistical Claims\n- **Tightness-DR correlation (SciERC):** rho=0.85 on 7 data points is reported but statistically underpowered (n=7). This should be flagged as suggestive rather than conclusive.\n- **Tightness-DR correlation (DocRED):** rho=0.14, p=0.18 in the unconstrained setting is a null result. The paper correctly reports this. However, the full S1 setting shows rho=0.52, p<0.001 on n=82 relations, which is more meaningful. The paper should more clearly distinguish between these two different analyses (unconstrained DR vs. residual DR after constraints).\n- **F1 neutrality:** The claim of \"zero F1 cost\" is based on R-F1 8.23% (unconstrained) vs. 8.36% (full S1) on SciERC. With ~1700 triples, this difference is not statistically significant, which is correctly acknowledged. But no confidence intervals or bootstrap tests are reported. Given that absolute F1 is ~8%, a 0.13pp difference could be within noise of a 1.5% relative change -- or nothing. The paper should report CIs.\n- **Cross-model correlation:** rho=0.89 for per-relation DR rank between Qwen and Llama is compelling but based on only 7 relations on SciERC. This needs validation on DocRED.\n\n### 3.4 Evaluation Methodology\n- **Metrics:** The four violation types are well-defined. F1 as a control variable (not optimization target) is correctly framed.\n- **Datasets:** SciERC (7 relations, 551 sentences) and DocRED (96 relations, 998 documents) provide good contrast in ontology complexity. However, both are English-only, academic/Wikipedia domains.\n- **Models:** Two models (Qwen3-8B, Llama-3.1-8B) at the same scale. No larger models tested. The grammar layer is plausibly scale-invariant, but the semantic layer's effectiveness at scale is unknown.\n- **JSON parse rate:** Llama's 92% parse rate (vs. Qwen's ~100%) means 8% of Llama's outputs are lost. The paper should discuss whether these unparseable outputs would have been high- or low-violation triples, and whether this introduces selection bias.\n\n### 3.5 Confounds and Missing Controls\n1. **Generation parameter sensitivity:** All experiments use a single set of generation parameters (temperature, top-k/p). Constrained decoding interacts with sampling strategy -- e.g., greedy decoding + constraints may behave very differently from nucleus sampling + constraints. No sensitivity analysis is provided.\n2. **Prompt sensitivity:** Only one prompt template per condition (minimal, schema-informed). The violation rates could be sensitive to prompt phrasing.\n3. **Single seed:** All experiments appear to use a single random seed. No variance estimates across seeds.\n4. **DocRED grammar-only DR anomaly:** DR jumps from 18.7% (unconstrained) to 26.17% (grammar-only). The paper explains this as a \"measurement artifact\" where grammar forces valid relation names, changing the triple distribution. This explanation is plausible but not rigorously verified. An alternative explanation is that grammar constraints distort the model's probability distribution in ways that increase DR -- consistent with the CRANE and Grammar-Aligned Decoding concerns cited in related work.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Results Interpretation\n\n### 4.1 Claims Supported by Evidence\n- Grammar layer universally eliminates ET and RV: **strongly supported** (0% across both datasets, both models).\n- Full S1 achieves 94% DR reduction on SciERC at zero F1 cost: **supported** (though single-seed, single-prompt caveats apply).\n- Enabling dependency between grammar and semantic layers: **supported** by the semantic-only ablation showing much weaker DR reduction.\n- Violation rates driven by constraint tightness: **supported** by the DocRED correlation analysis (rho=0.52), though the SciERC correlation (rho=0.85, n=7) is underpowered.\n\n### 4.2 Negative Results\n- DocRED DR reduction (46% vs. SciERC's 94%): The paper handles this well by reframing as \"predictable partial reduction\" governed by constraint tightness. The bimodal distribution analysis (67% of relations achieve <2% DR) is a strong addition.\n- The tightness-DR null result on unconstrained DocRED (rho=0.14, p=0.18) is appropriately reported. The paper correctly notes that tightness predicts *residual* violations after constraints, not unconstrained violations (where model priors dominate).\n\n### 4.3 Enabling Dependency Analysis\nThe enabling ratio metric (full_S1 DR reduction / semantic-only DR reduction) is a useful quantification. The argument that grammar is a prerequisite for semantic (not vice versa) is well-supported: semantic-only leaves RV/ET intact, meaning ~29% of DocRED triples have unknown relation types for which no domain/range rules exist. This is a convincing mechanistic explanation.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Weaknesses\n\n1. **CF violation trade-off is under-addressed.** SciERC CF rises from <0.1% to 9.9% with full S1. While the paper argues CF violations are \"soft preferences\" vs. DR violations as \"hard constraints,\" a 10% CF rate means 1 in 10 entities receives inconsistent type assignments. For knowledge graph construction, this creates contradictory facts. The paper acknowledges this as a limitation but does not quantify the downstream impact (e.g., on KG consistency, query answering).\n\n2. **No post-hoc baseline comparison.** The paper's argument against post-hoc filtering is entirely theoretical. A simple baseline -- generate unconstrained, filter invalid triples, report coverage and F1 of remaining triples -- would directly demonstrate the value of decode-time enforcement over the naive alternative. This is a significant omission.\n\n3. **DocRED grammar-only DR increase lacks rigorous explanation.** DR jumping from 18.7% to 26.17% when adding grammar-only constraints is counter-intuitive and potentially damaging to the paper's narrative. If grammar constraints can *increase* one violation type while eliminating others, this suggests constraint interactions that the paper's composition framework (simple bitwise AND) does not account for. The paper needs a more thorough analysis of which triples are gained/lost and why their DR is higher.\n\n4. **Limited model diversity.** Two 8B-parameter models at the same scale is minimal for a claim of model-agnostic effectiveness. Larger models (70B+) may have different type-assignment priors, and the semantic layer's effectiveness could scale differently. Instruction-tuned vs. base model comparison is also absent.\n\n5. **Absolute F1 is very low (~8%).** While the paper correctly frames F1 as a control variable, the 8% relation F1 means the system is extracting very few correct triples. Constraint enforcement on a system that produces 92% incorrect triples (by F1) has limited practical value. The paper should discuss whether constraints become more or less useful as base extraction quality improves (e.g., with few-shot or fine-tuned models).\n\n6. **Single prompt template, single seed, no variance estimates.** The absence of any variance measurement weakens all quantitative claims. Even reporting results over 3 random seeds with different sampling would substantially strengthen the paper.\n\n7. **Tightness metric ignores distributional priors.** The current metric treats all entity type pairs as equally likely. A distribution-aware tightness metric (weighted by the model's actual type generation probabilities) would likely show stronger correlation and provide better predictive value. The moderate rho=0.52 partially reflects this limitation.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. What happens when you combine schema-informed prompting with constrained decoding? Does the prompt-level \"pre-filtering\" help or hurt when combined with decode-time enforcement? The Evaluate-for regression (+5.8pp with prompt) is concerning -- does this regression persist under constrained decoding?\n\n2. For the DocRED grammar-only DR increase (18.7% to 26.17%): can you provide a breakdown of which specific triples are responsible? Specifically, are these triples that were previously RV violations (and thus excluded from DR computation) but are now grammar-forced into valid relation names (and thus newly included in DR computation)? If so, this is a measurement artifact, not a real increase -- but the paper should make this explicit with numbers.\n\n3. The graceful degradation fallback (semantic mask intersected with grammar mask = empty) occurred 1/998 times on DocRED. On an ontology with mean tightness 0.95 (vs. DocRED's 0.842), how frequently would you expect this fallback? Is there a tightness threshold beyond which the semantic layer becomes essentially inoperative?\n\n4. Have you measured CF violation rates for Llama-3.1-8B? The cross-model comparison (Table 5 in the paper) reports DR but not CF for Llama. If CF rates differ substantially between models, this would challenge the \"CF is driven by type system ambiguity\" hypothesis.\n\n5. Can you report bootstrap confidence intervals for the F1 numbers? With ~1700 triples, even a rough CI would help distinguish signal from noise.\n\n6. The paper reports relation F1 but not entity F1 consistently across all configurations. Table 8 (SciERC ablation) shows R-F1 but not E-F1 for all modes. Is entity extraction quality affected by constraints?\n\n7. What is the behavior of the semantic layer when the model generates an entity not present in the entity registry (e.g., a hallucinated entity that was not in the entity list section)? Does the semantic layer have a fallback for unregistered entities?\n\n---\n\n## 7. Minor Issues\n\n1. **Table inconsistencies:** The paper/prompt summary references Tables 1-11, but the LaTeX source shows Tables labeled by content (landscape, grammar, scierc, docred, etc.). The numbering in the prompt summary and the actual paper seem misaligned. Ensure consistent table numbering.\n\n2. **Notation:** The constraint mapping is defined as $D: R \\to 2^E \\times 2^E$ in the paper but as $\\sigma: R \\to P(E \\times E)$ in the writing plan. The paper should use consistent notation.\n\n3. **\"Near-Complete\" in title:** This is appropriate for SciERC (94% reduction) but potentially misleading for DocRED (46% reduction). Consider qualifying in the abstract more prominently.\n\n4. **Grammar-aligned decoding concern:** The paper cites CRANE (ICML'25) and Grammar-Aligned Decoding (NeurIPS'24) as risks but does not discuss whether the DocRED grammar-only DR increase is an instance of the \"naive masking distorts distribution\" problem these papers identify. This connection should be made explicit.\n\n5. **Related work gaps:** The paper does not cite recent work on LLM-based KG completion with ontology constraints (e.g., neurosymbolic approaches combining LLMs with description logic reasoners). These are conceptually adjacent.\n\n6. Missing bibliography -- the LaTeX source has `\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}` but no `\\bibliography{...}` command. No references are rendered.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Score Justification: 4 (Weak Accept)\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Well-defined problem with practical relevance (ontology compliance in LLM-based IE)\n- Clean violation taxonomy and decidability classification\n- Grammar layer results are convincing and universally effective (ET/RV to 0%)\n- Good experimental design with proper ablation on SciERC\n- Honest treatment of negative results (DocRED weaker reduction, tightness null result on unconstrained)\n- Tightness-violation correlation as a predictive tool is a useful practical contribution\n- The enabling dependency analysis is mechanistically convincing\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- CF trade-off (up to 9.9%) is a significant limitation that somewhat undermines the \"type-valid\" claim\n- No post-hoc filtering baseline for direct comparison\n- DocRED grammar-only DR increase is inadequately explained\n- Limited model diversity (two 8B models)\n- No variance estimates (single seed, single prompt)\n- Very low absolute F1 (~8%) raises questions about practical utility\n- Moderate correlation (rho=0.52) for the tightness predictor\n\nThe paper makes a solid contribution by (1) systematically measuring a real problem (ontology violations in LLM IE), (2) providing a clean and practical grammar-layer solution for vocabulary/type violations, and (3) proposing a reasonable (if imperfect) semantic-layer approach for domain/range violations. The tightness analysis adds analytical depth. However, the CF trade-off, limited model diversity, absence of variance estimates, and lack of a post-hoc baseline comparison prevent a stronger recommendation. The work is above the acceptance threshold for a venue like EMNLP but would benefit from addressing the post-hoc baseline and CF trade-off analysis in revision.\n\n**Confidence:** 4 (Confident but not certain)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7980,"cache_read_input_tokens":64192,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7980,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01E5qdXDrgD7MQ7cvXgf66Ae","session_id":"8810d8ca-95df-4cbf-a0e9-6349587d15d7","uuid":"e51e09a8-13e9-43ac-a18c-d3c1431c7bac"}
